{
  "platform_id": "aXmKAsE3_Ec",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:aXmKAsE3_Ec",
    "purpose": "A side-by-side test of three kinds of non-stick pan: a Teflon-style coated aluminum pan, a vintage well-seasoned Wagner cast iron pan, and the Our Place Titanium Pro, which Kenji co-developed. The tests are heating evenness, steak searing, cleanup, salmon skin searing, fried eggs and omelettes, so viewers can see where each pan does well and pick one for how they cook.",
    "video_type": "comparison",
    "thesis": "No pan material is magic and each involves trade-offs. Kenji runs the same tests on all three pans so viewers can judge each one. He openly says the Titanium Pro is his own collaboration product.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Heating evenness is tested with tortillas and cheese over a ring burner. The pure aluminum Teflon pan heats most evenly, cast iron shows clear hot spots, and the clad Titanium Pro comes out close to aluminum.",
      "Teflon breaks down at roughly 450–475°F and can give off harmful fumes, so it is not used for high-heat searing.",
      "Cast iron needs a long preheat of about 10 minutes to sear evenly. When preheated properly it gives the darkest crust.",
      "Cleanup covers soap on seasoned cast iron, drying and re-oiling cast iron, soaking, air drying and dishwasher use. The Titanium Pro's surface gradually darkens or seasons with use, and this is normal.",
      "Because cast iron stores more heat, the same heat setting and timing gives different results than in other pans, so cooking times have to be adjusted for each pan.",
      "The fried egg slide test and the omelette test compare how well each surface releases food. The omelette uses a water-evaporation method to bring the pan to about 230°F before the butter goes in.",
      "Metal utensils are tested on each surface: Teflon needs non-metal tools, while cast iron and the Titanium Pro are used with a metal fish spatula and fork.",
      "An older Teflon pan with scratches illustrates that coatings wear out over a few years even with careful use."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction to pans",
        "summary": "Kenji introduces the three pans: a Teflon-style coated pan, a vintage seasoned Wagner cast iron pan, and the Our Place Titanium Pro. He discloses that he partnered with Our Place on the Titanium Pro and explains the side-by-side testing approach.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Even heating test",
        "summary": "Tortillas topped with cheese are heated on a ring-flame burner to map each pan's heat distribution. He explains how aluminum, steel/iron and clad construction conduct heat.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Searing steak test",
        "summary": "Unseasoned steaks are seared for 60 seconds per side in each pan. Teflon's temperature limits and fumes are discussed, and the cast iron is retested after a proper preheat.",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "last_segment": 16
      },
      {
        "title": "Cleanup comparison",
        "summary": "Kenji washes each pan and covers soap on seasoned cast iron, drying and re-seasoning cast iron, soaking, air drying, dishwasher safety, and the Titanium Pro's surface gradually seasoning.",
        "first_segment": 17,
        "last_segment": 23
      },
      {
        "title": "Searing salmon test",
        "summary": "Skin-on salmon is seared at medium heat under a chef's press to compare crispness, evenness and release. The cast iron needs a shorter time because it stores more heat, and he contrasts silicone spatulas with metal fish spatulas.",
        "first_segment": 23,
        "last_segment": 30
      },
      {
        "title": "Fried egg test",
        "summary": "Eggs are fried with a one-second spray of cooking spray, and each pan gets a slide test to judge how slick it is. This includes a joking 'slickness scale' of TV and film characters.",
        "first_segment": 31,
        "last_segment": 35
      },
      {
        "title": "Omelet test",
        "summary": "Omelettes are made with the water-evaporation method to set the pan temperature, then compared for sticking, shape and texture. Pan shape and weight are also discussed.",
        "first_segment": 35,
        "last_segment": 43
      },
      {
        "title": "Utensils, Teflon wear and conclusion",
        "summary": "Kenji compares how each surface tolerates metal utensils and shows a scratched, retired five-year-old Teflon pan. He then sums up the trade-offs of the three pans and why he partnered with Our Place.",
        "first_segment": 43,
        "last_segment": 46
      }
    ],
    "passages": [
